  "text": "Question fifteen. How do you choose a color palette? Match it to the data. Categories get distinct hues. Ordered values get one hue, from light to dark. Values with a meaningful middle, like profit and loss, get two hues that meet at a neutral center. And save one bold accent for the series you want them to see. Question sixteen. Why cap a categorical palette at six or eight colors? Because past that, the palette runs out and starts again, and two unrelated groups share a color. Fold the tail into other, or split the chart into small panels. Question seventeen. How do you make a chart accessible? About eight percent of men have red-green color blindness, so don't carry meaning by color alone. Here are red and green as most people see them, and as a red-green colorblind reader sees them. Add a second cue, like a direct label, and check the chart in grayscale. If it still reads, it's robust.",
